
<% content_for :title, "Anger & Conflict Relapse Plan | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="relapse-plan-page relapse-plan-anger">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-hero">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <%= link_to "← ALL RELAPSE PLANS",
          toolbox_relapse_plans_path,
          class: "relapse-plan-back" %>

      <p class="relapse-plan-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / RELAPSE PLANS / ANGER &amp; CONFLICT
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>PISSED OFF.</span>
        <span>STILL</span>
        <span>SOBER.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="relapse-plan-intro">
        YOUR SOBRIETY PLAN FOR ARGUMENTS,
        RESENTMENT &amp; SEEING RED.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / YOUR EXPERIENCE -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-purple">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        01 / YOUR EXPERIENCE
      </p>

      <h2>
        ANGER ENTERS.<br>
        THEN WHAT?
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-copy">

        <p>
          When you get angry, everything can feel personal,
          urgent, and completely justified. Your thoughts
          speed up, your patience disappears, and suddenly
          you have a very convincing argument for why
          someone else is the problem.
        </p>

        <p>
          Bipolar irritability can turn the volume up,
          but not every frustration is a symptom.
          Sometimes you have a legitimate reason
          to be angry. The problem begins when
          anger starts making decisions for you.
        </p>

        <p>
          Drinking can start looking like an escape.
          You might want to shut your thoughts off,
          stop caring, punish yourself, or turn
          an awful evening into something different.
          Alcohol promises distance from the anger,
          but it also lowers your inhibitions
          and makes impulsive decisions easier.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't need to resolve the argument
          immediately. You don't need the other
          person to admit you're right.
          And you definitely don't need to drink
          because someone pissed you off.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            You can be furious, have a legitimate
            grievance, and still be responsible
            for what you do next.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / YOUR BUILDUP -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-black">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        02 / YOUR BUILDUP
      </p>

      <h2>
        BEFORE THE<br>
        "FUCK YOU."
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>WHAT SETS YOU OFF</h3>

          <p>
            Feeling disrespected, dismissed, ignored,
            controlled, or treated unfairly.
            People breaking commitments, crossing
            boundaries, or making decisions that
            affect you without considering you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pain, exhaustion, stress, boredom,
            and overstimulation can make your
            tolerance even lower. Sometimes
            you're reacting to the immediate
            situation; sometimes resentment
            has been building for days.
          </p>

        </div>


        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>WHAT YOU NOTICE FIRST</h3>

          <p>
            Your jaw tightens. Your body feels tense.
            You start mentally rehearsing arguments,
            replaying what someone said,
            and constructing increasingly
            convincing responses.
          </p>

          <p>
            You interrupt, become sarcastic,
            raise your voice, or feel compelled
            to send a message immediately.
            Your thinking becomes absolute:
            always, never, everyone, no one.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you're sleeping less, feeling
            unusually energized, or getting
            irritated by everything, consider
            whether your mood is changing too.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / YOUR CONFLICT PLAN -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-yellow">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        03 / YOUR CONFLICT PLAN
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHAT DO YOU DO<br>
        WITH THIS?
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR SPACE &amp; SAFETY PLAN</h3>

          <p>
            When you notice yourself escalating,
            stop the conversation. Put down your
            phone, step away, and give yourself
            at least 20 minutes before reengaging. 
            
          </p>

          <p>
            Don't follow someone around to finish
            an argument. Don't send a series
            of messages, threaten, insult,
            or try to force an immediate resolution.
          </p>

          <p>
            Change rooms, listen to music,
            sit somewhere quiet, or use
            cold water to help settle your body.
            Move your body, go for a walk.
            Don't drive while overwhelmed.
          </p>


        </div>


        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>WORDS YOU CAN USE</h3>

          <p>
            "I'm too angry to have
            this conversation productively.
            I'll come back to it later."
          </p>

          <p>
            "I need some space.
            I'm not ignoring the problem;
            I'm choosing not to make
            it worse."
          </p>

          <p>
            "I'm upset about what happened,
            but I'm not going to argue
            while I'm this worked up."
          </p>

          <p>
            "I can discuss this when
            we're both ready to listen."
          </p>

          <p>
            You can set a boundary without
            delivering a speech, proving
            your point, or getting
            the last word.
          </p>

        </div>


        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>THE DRINKING URGE</h3>

          <p>
            If anger turns into wanting alcohol,
            name what's happening: you're angry,
            you want relief, and drinking is
            the solution your brain is offering.
          </p>

          <p>
            Play the tape forward. Alcohol might
            temporarily change how you feel,
            but it won't resolve the conflict.
            It can make you more impulsive,
            less restrained, and more likely
            to create additional consequences.
          </p>

          <p>
            Don't go to the liquor store,
            browse alcohol delivery, or put
            yourself somewhere drinking
            is the easiest option.
          </p>

          <p>
            Eat something, drink water,
            change your surroundings, go outside, move your body,
            and give the urge time to change.
            If it keeps getting stronger,
            contact someone in your recovery
            network or follow your
            established relapse plan.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>
              Someone else's behavior
              doesn't get to determine
              whether you stay sober.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>


        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>WHO YOU CAN CALL</h3>

          <p>
            Your first choice is someone
            who can listen without encouraging
            you to escalate the situation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Contact a trusted friend or
            recovery peer.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can say:
            "I'm really angry, and it's
            making me want to drink.
            Can you talk with me
            until I settle down?"
          </p>

          <p>
            If no one answers, try another
            person or a meeting.
            You don't need to handle
            a serious craving alone.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / AFTER THE HEAT -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-pink">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        04 / AFTER THE HEAT
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHAT STILL<br>
        NEEDS ATTENTION?
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-copy">

        <p>
          Once the intensity has passed,
          examine what actually happened.
          What was the conflict?
          What did you assume?
          What did you know?
          What part of your reaction was
          justified, and where
          did you go too far?
        </p>

        <p>
          If you hurt someone, acknowledge
          it and make repairs. If someone
          crossed a boundary, address it
          without pretending it was acceptable.
          You don't have to choose between
          accountability and self-respect.
        </p>

        <p>
          Check your sobriety too.
          Did you experience a craving?
          Did you start making excuses
          for drinking? What helped,
          and what would you change
          next time?
        </p>

        <p>
          If your anger has become unusually
          frequent, intense, or difficult
          to control, especially alongside
          changes in sleep, energy,
          or impulsivity, bring that
          information to your psych.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't have to forgive
          everyone immediately, resolve
          every disagreement, or pretend
          you're no longer angry.
          Some problems require boundaries.
          Others require time.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            The argument can remain unresolved.
            The anger can take time to fade.
            Your sobriety doesn't have
            to be part of the damage.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

</main>
